.TH LOGGUARD 8 "2026" "logguard 1.0.5" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
logguard \- keep /var/log from filling up
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B logguard
[\fB\-c\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-\-status\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B logguard
checks the free space (blocks and inodes, whichever is lower) of
.IR LOG_DIR .
It is meant for boards whose /var/log lives on zram or tmpfs. It is run every
minute by
.BR logguard.timer (5).
.PP
When free space drops below
.B MIN_FREE_PERCENT
it moves rotated and stale logs to a persistent archive directory, runs
.B journalctl \-\-vacuum\-size
and, as a last resort, saves the tail of the biggest active text logs and
truncates them, stopping as soon as
.B TARGET_FREE_PERCENT
is reached. Archived batches are deleted after
.B RETENTION_HOURS
(24 by default). Binary files are never truncated and files open by a running
process are never moved.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Configuration file (default /etc/logguard.conf).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be done without touching anything.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose output.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print free space and archived batches, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 3 if free space is still below the minimum after cleaning.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/logguard.conf
Configuration (KEY=VALUE).
.TP
.I /var/lib/logguard/archive/<timestamp>/
Archived batches.
.SH AUTHOR
mgoreiro <mgoreiro@gmail.com>. Project page: https://github.com/mgoreiro/logguard
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR logrotate (8),
.BR journalctl (1)
